it's thought to be the world's most  powerful volcanic eruption in 30 years  the explosion of the hungar tonga hunga  ha upai volcano on saturday caused a  sonic boom that could be heard six  thousand miles away in alaska and  cloaked part of the pacific in ash  this afternoon the brother of missing  british woman angela glover confirmed  she'd been found dead it's reported she  was swept away by this tsunami wave that  hit the coast of tonga 20 minutes after  the eruption  she's beautiful she was absolutely a ray  of sunshine um that  she would walk into a room and just  lighten the room up and she  loved her life both when she was working  in london and then she achieved her life  stream of going to work in the south  pacific  the tsunami is expected to have caused  extensive damage to low-lying parts of  tonga but details are scarce the  submarine cable that connects the  islands to the rest of the world has  been damaged  the cloud of volcanic ash is blocking  satellite communications  we'd be able to make contact with our  teams on the ground through things like  satellite phone technology but because  of the ash cloud even that kind of  communication has been severely impacted  we have not had uh con  contact with our red cross team on the  ground since saturday afternoon just  after the eruption itself  it's difficult to imagine the impact on  tonga given the tsunami caused damage  along the coast of south america  two people reportedly drowned off a  beach in peru in unexpectedly high waves  and the eruption more like a detonation  sent shockwaves literally around the  world  these satellite images show the pressure  wave moving at the speed of sound away  from the pacific  the eruption was so powerful those shock  waves were being detected on pressure  gauges at weather stations like this one  around the world in fact faint ripples  are still passing through the atmosphere  more than two days after the event  so we saw the first wave and then we  probably saw the second wave as it  reflected back and during the course of  today we've probably seen another couple  of waves as it's gone back and forth  just like ripples in the swimming pool  i've been looking at pressure records  for nine fifty years and i've never seen  anything as strong as this  instruments around the world witnessed  the eruption in real time but until  reconnaissance flights from new zealand  and australia return the outside world  has little idea how much tonga has  suffered and how much help it will need  tom clark sky news
 

